# The New Year

The next year is very much upon us, I am hopeful that for SquirrelJME's tenth
year that SquirrelJME 0.4.0 is released at some point in 2026. However, in
the meantime there have been a number of changes and updates which have
occured.

Since November, I have been slowly working on the new build pipeline so that
SquirrelJME builds are completely self-hosted and self-reliant. Currently as
of now, the majority of the can be performed under CMake which naturally
calls Gradle and CMake, again. The ultimate goal here is to eventually
migrate everything onto [Concourse](https://concourse-ci.org/) as that
supports Fossil, is open-source, and can be self-hosted.

For the release build, ProGuard is used to optimize the code by shrinking and
removing everything that is not needed. Due to ProGuard being rather buggy,
it often times would just run out of memory resulting in failed builds
especially on 32-bit systems or macOS. It will now be attempted twice, and
if both times fail it will instead be skipped.

Generic raster pixel format translation has been added so SquirrelJME is now
capable of converting to and from any pixel format to any other pixel format.
This means that any APIs and devices which use a specific pixel format are now
better supported.

Additionally, _AShiningRay_, who has worked on FreeJ2ME-Plus, has contributed
support for Nokia's `DirectGraphics` APIs so SquirrelJME is now able to play
some games such as _Bounce Tales_, with some minor transparency/alpha related
issues. _AShiningRay_ has also fixed a number of issues with transformations,
which games such as _Sonic_ render properly. On top of this as well, there
is now support for drawing polygons, arcs, and rounded rectangles. Currently,
triangle drawing uses the polygon drawing which is non-optimal however it is
a stand-in until a proper triangle rendering algorithm is implemented. Copying
of regions has been implemented as well.

From these contributions, I have utilized _AShiningRay_'s work to add
polygon, triangle, arcs, and rounded rectangle drawing support to DoJa.

There is now support for more blending modes on top of SRC and SRC_OVER, all
the rest of them are supported now albeit they are only going to be seen
in very specific cases. This is mostly a concern for my future plans to
support AWT and eventually Java applets (people still remember those, right?).

Dependencies for the Debian packages has been fixed as well! These packages
should now install properly for Debian and/or Ubuntu.

Regarding Flatpak, it was asked if there is an alternative, I have been
considering AppImages which would work on platforms such as the Steam Deck
and any Linux distro. I am however, still evaluating this.

NanoCoat now has better native path handling support, it is able to handle
POSIX, DOS, VFAT, and Windows paths in a uniform and well-defined way. This
will be integrated into `Path` eventually.

OSS is now always enabled on POSIX platforms such as BSD and Linux, even if
your system does not include any built-in OSS headers. Thus, you should always
be able to get sound assuming you have OSS or a compatibility layer such
as `osspd` installed.

ScritchUI now better handles DPI on GTK2 and Win32, and also is DPI aware
on Win32, so it should look much better and be more accurate without
Windows performing scaling for you. On top of this, there is generic
DPI awareness in ScritchUI for when other platforms are eventually added. A
bug was fixed as well, where if the height was requested for a display it
would instead return the width.

ScritchAudio now correctly initializes U8 buffers to the proper zero level,
so if you happen to be using the worst audio format possible it will now
sound a bit better. Additionally, there will be a slight boost to speed as
it will no longer mix in the first stream of audio if it is pointless to do.

For NanoCoat, there have also been important fixes regarding ILP32, ILP64,
and ILLP64 platforms. MIPSr6 received a few compilation fixes in NanoCoat as
it is now properly detected as a unique processor architecture.

DoJa also had a tendency to not work properly since a refactor due to changes
in how application properties were handled, this should be fixed.
